{
  "_comment": "Top-level match_nodes whose patterns[0] cannot honestly be a clean literal. Read by scripts/check-canonical-names.py: listed nodes are reported but do not fail; a stale entry (the node is fixed, renamed or gone) fails, so a fix must delete its entry. None of these can be fixed in the registry alone -- each category needs a resolver change, described under 'categories'.",
  "categories": {
    "dotted-name": "The source's own name contains '.' (ITU-T X.805, IEEE 802.11). SecID-Service extractNameSlug accepts only [A-Za-z0-9_-], so the correct literal is rejected. Renaming to 'x-805' would break 'follow the source'. Fix: let extractNameSlug accept '.'.",
    "family-template": "One node matches a numbered series (GLI-11, GLI-19; YD/T 1234; NIST 800-53). No single literal names every member, and a family literal would make every member's canonical SecID the family name. Fix: when patterns[0] is not a literal, echo the matched name instead of slugifying the description.",
    "name-is-identifier": "The name is itself an open identifier (arXiv ID, DOI, ISBN, commit SHA). The canonical form is the input. Same fix as family-template."
  },
